malcolmr.google@cse.unsw.edu.au (Malcolm Ryan) wrote in message news:<e6fd417e.0407071717.7711c2f8@posting.google.com>...
> While I understand and respect your argument, in my opinion this is a
> _feature_ of NetHack, and not a limitation. Or rather, it is a
> limitation, but the choice to limit oneself is part of artistic
> expression. Like when a writer chooses to write sonnets, or an artist
> chooses to paint in oils on canvas. What makes NetHack most appealing
> to me is that it has deliberately limited itself to ASCII text in an
> 80x25 window, and yet has explored that limited medium and produced
> something quite remarkable.

I like this explanation, and agree with it mostly.  However, after a
while of working under one limitation, while working under it, it
becomes harder and harder to come up with new, original expressions
under that limitation.  With formal poetry we're talking about
language, where even the change of a single word can have a profound
effect on the entire work.  Nethack levels are composed of a bunch of
spaces, and changing the contents of one square on the grid most often
does not provide such richness.

In other words, eventually people run out of ideas, and the boundries
will have to be pushed back (by increasing level size, adding
monsters, new tile types, or making something weird like rolling
boulder traps) to create more interesting things.  I don't know if
that's happened yet (my hat's off to the guy who created the Gnome
King's Apiary), but now that we have so many special levels floating
about it'll just come about that much faster.

